(a) It is the policy of the city that the proper operation of democratic government requires that public officials be independent, impartial and responsible to the people; that governmental decisions and policy be made in proper channels of the governmental structure; that public office not be used for personal gain; and that the public have confidence in the integrity of its government. In recognition of these goals, a code of ethics for all city officials, employees, and appointees is adopted.
(b) This Code has the following purposes:
(1) To encourage high ethical standards in official conduct by city officials, employees, and appointees;
(2) To establish guidelines for ethical standards of conduct for all such city officials, employees, and appointees by setting forth those acts or actions that are incompatible with the best interest of the city;
(3) To require disclosure by such officials of private financial or other interest in manners affecting the city;
(4) To serve as a basis for disciplining those who refuse to abide by its terms; and
(5) To maintain the highest standards of employees, and elected and appointed officials, including to serve others, not themselves, to use resources with efficiency and economy, treat all people fairly, use the power of their position for the well-being of the constituents, and create an environment of honesty, openness and integrity.
(c) It is the policy of the city that ethics complaints filed under this Code be adjudicated on their merits in the interest of justice and to afford all parties access to the remedies hereunder. Therefore, to the greatest extent possible, complaints shall not be dismissed for purely procedural or technical reasons.
(Ord. No. 2020-05, § 1, 4-20-20; Ord. No. 2025-03, § 1(Exh. A), 2-3-25)
